      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2014 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "INTRODUCTION",
            "content": "Kidney transplantation is the treatment of choice for ESRD. Several studies have investigated the factors that may affect kidney function at 1 year. The factors mentioned are anemia, hypercholesterolemia, immunosuppressors, etc. We studied the independent predictors of serum creatinine>100μmol/L at 1 year."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
            "content": "We conducted a retrospective study from March 1999 to December 2009. We conducted a chart review of 402 kidney transplant patients. The kidneys were removed from cadaveric donors. Data collected included age, weight, height, preoperative BMI, the causal nephropathy, smoking, hypertension, diabetes, anticoagulation. Intraoperative data included operative time, and cold ischemia. Statistical analysis consisted of a t-test for independent samples comparing the group with a creatinine≤100μmol/L vs>100 group, and univariate and multivariate Cox regression for a serum creatinine>100μmol/L at 1 year and test of correlation between BMI and serum creatinine at 1 year postoperatively."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We found a significant difference in BMI and cold ischemia with P=0.008 and P=0.002, respectively. In contrast there was no difference in age, operative time and blood loss, P=0.758, P=0.941 and P=0.963, respectively. Multivariate Cox regression showed that donor age P=0.004 (HR: 1.016 and CI: 1.005-1.027), a recipient age P=0.023 (HR: 0.986 and CI: 0.974-0.998) and BMI P=0.001 (HR: 1.019 and CI: 1.010-1.028) were independent predictors of serum creatinine>100μmol/L at 1 year. The Pearson correlation coefficient r=0.154 (P=0.004) showed a significant correlation between BMI and serum creatinine."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Our study showed that donor age, recipient age and BMI were independent predictors of renal function>100μmol/L at 1 year. Our results highlight the difficulty of the management of obesity in renal transplant patients."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "LEVEL OF EVIDENCE",
            "content": 5
          }
        ]
      },
